Article 24
(1) The Regency Regional Government undertakes prevention efforts as referred to in Article 23 paragraph (3) letter a, to avoid Women from the impacts of Conflict situations and events.
(2) The Regency Regional Government in undertaking prevention efforts as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be conducted by:
a. providing data and studies regarding women in Conflict;
b. increasing public awareness, customary institutions or customary and social institutions, interfaith communication forums to provide protection for women in Conflict;
c. increasing the role of mass media in providing dissemination and information on the protection of women in Conflict;
d. increasing the role of women's service units to provide protection for women in Conflict;
e. conducting training in the prevention of Violence against women in Conflict; and
f. facilitating the addition of provision of public spaces/urban green open spaces for women.

Article 71
Prevention of VAC involving parties in the Regency Area as referred to in Article 70 letter a is implemented through:
a. implementation of policies, programs, and activities for VAC prevention involving parties in the Regency Area;
b. provision of education on VAC prevention activities involving parties in the Regency Area;
c. enhancement of the participation of the Community, Business Sector, media, and Children in VAC prevention in the Regency Area; and
d. strengthening of cooperation to realize CFC, child-friendly sub-districts, and child-friendly Villages/urban villages.

Article 32
The Regent coordinates the Protection of Women from GBV in disasters in the Regency Area, as referred to in Article 31 paragraph (1), carried out by means of:
a. providing information and Disaggregated Data based on gender, age, and type of GBV vulnerability;
b. building stakeholder commitment to the prevention, handling, and Protection of Women from GBV in disasters;
c. providing women-friendly space facilities, as well as Gender Responsive infrastructure and facilities in emergency response and rehabilitation conditions that accommodate local resources;
d. providing complaint, handling, and referral services needed by women victims of Violence that accommodate local wisdom; and
e. ensuring victims or survivors receive empowerment services according to their potential, abilities, and needs so they can quickly recover, become independent, and productive.

Article 43
(1) The provision of services for women victims of Violence requiring coordination in the Regency Region as referred to in Article 37 letter b shall be implemented in accordance with standards and procedures.
(2) The standards for provision of services for women victims of Violence requiring coordination in the Regency Region as referred to in paragraph (1) include:
a. receiving reports and/or outreach to victims of Violence;
b. providing information about the rights of victims of Violence;
c. facilitating the provision of health services;
d. facilitating the provision of psychological strengthening services;
e. facilitating the provision of psychosocial services, Social Rehabilitation, social empowerment, Repatriation, and Social Reintegration;
f. providing legal services;
g. identifying economic empowerment needs;
h. identifying temporary shelter needs for victims of Violence and Families of victims of Violence that need to be fulfilled immediately;
i. facilitating the needs of Victims of Violence with disabilities;
j. coordination and cooperation for the fulfillment of the rights of victims of Violence with other institutions; and
k. monitoring the fulfillment of the rights of victims of Violence by law enforcement apparatus during the judicial process.
(3) The procedures for provision of services as referred to in paragraph (1) consist of:
a. provision of public complaint services for women victims of Violence in the Regency Region, including:
1. establishment of UPTD PPA in the Regency Region;
2. implementation of UPTD PPA services; and
3. provision and strengthening of human resources of service providers through training and certification of Women's rights protection services.
b. coordination and synchronization of the implementation of provision of further referral services for women victims of violence in the Regency Region, including:
1. cross-coordination of Regency Regional Apparatus for the implementation of provision of KTP case services;
2. development of policies and implementation of an integrated KTP case handling system across related Regency Regional Apparatus; and
3. data collection, monitoring, and evaluation of KTP case handling.

Article 46
Strengthening and development of Women's Protection Service Provider Institutions in Regency Areas as referred to in Article 37 letter c shall be implemented through:
a. capacity building of human resources of Women's Protection Service Provider Institutions for women victims of Violence in Regency Areas;
b. provision of specific needs for women in emergency situations and special conditions in Regency Areas;
c. development of communication, information, and education strategies regarding the protection of women's rights in Regency Areas;
d. strengthening networks among Women's Protection Service Provider Institutions in Regency Areas;
e. facilitation of efforts to fulfill standards of Women's Protection Service Provider Institutions in Regency Areas; and
f. development of Women's Protection Service Provider Institutions in Regency Areas.

Article 42
The provision of services for women victims of Violence requiring coordination in the Regency Area as referred to in Article 37 letter b, shall be implemented through:
a. receiving reports and/or outreach to Victims of Violence;
b. providing information about the rights of Victims of Violence;
c. facilitating the provision of health services;
d. facilitating the provision of psychological strengthening services;
e. facilitating the provision of psychosocial services, Social Rehabilitation, social empowerment, Repatriation, and Social Reintegration;
f. providing legal services;
g. identifying economic empowerment needs;
h. identifying temporary shelter needs for Victims of Violence and Families of Victims of Violence that need to be fulfilled immediately;
i. facilitating the needs of Victims of Violence with disabilities;
j. coordinating and cooperating on the fulfillment of the rights of Victims of Violence with other institutions; and
k. monitoring the fulfillment of the rights of victims of Violence by law enforcement apparatus during the judicial proceedings.

Article 47
(1) The strengthening and development of Women's Protection Service Provider Institutions in Regency Areas as referred to in Article 37 letter c shall be implemented in accordance with standards and procedures.
(2) The standards for strengthening and development as referred to in paragraph (1) include:
a. measurable and certified human resource capacity of Women's Protection Service Provider Institutions for women victims of Violence in Regency Areas;
b. specific needs for women in emergency situations and special conditions in Regency Areas that are well-targeted and effective;
c. effective and efficient communication, information, and education strategies regarding the protection of women's rights;
d. networks among Women's Protection Service Provider Institutions in Regency Areas;
e. Women's Protection Service Provider Institutions in Regency Areas have agreed service standards and operational system procedures; and
f. Measurable development of Women's Protection Service Provider Institutions in Regency Areas.
(3) The procedures for strengthening and development as referred to in paragraph (1) include:
a. policy advocacy and assistance in providing service facilities and infrastructure for women victims of Violence in Regency Areas;
b. capacity building of institutional resources of service provider institutions for handling women victims of Violence in Regency Areas;
c. provision of specific needs for women in emergency situations and special conditions in Regency Areas; and
d. strengthening networks among Women's Protection Service Provider Institutions in Regency Areas.

Article 31
(1) The Regent coordinates the implementation of Protection of Women from GBV in disasters.
(2) The coordination of implementation of Protection of Women from GBV in disasters as referred to in paragraph (1), is carried out by:
a. ensuring the existence of programs and activities conducted by Regency Regional Apparatus regarding the protection of women's rights from GBV in disasters;
b. ensuring the fulfillment of specific needs of women including persons with disabilities and the elderly;
c. monitoring the development of services related to victim handling;
d. establishing a mechanism for implementing monitoring, evaluation, and reporting of Protection of Women from GBV services in disasters periodically and in a tiered manner; and
e. formulating an action plan for Protection of Women from GBV in disasters.

Article 29
(1) Every Woman in the Regency Area as a Victim of domestic violence as referred to in Article 27 letter b, has the right to receive:
a. protection from the Family, police, prosecutor's office, court, advocate, social institution, or other parties either temporarily or based on the determination of a protection order from the court;
b. health services in accordance with medical needs;
c. special handling related to the confidentiality of victims;
d. assistance by social workers and legal aid at every level of the examination process in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations; and
e. spiritual guidance services.
(2) The granting of rights to women in the Regency Area as Victims of domestic violence as referred to in paragraph (1), shall be implemented in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations governing the elimination of domestic violence.

Article 25
(1) The Regency Regional Government provides services to women as referred to in Article 23 paragraph (3) letter b.
(2) The provision of services as referred to in paragraph (1), is given to:
a. women victims as a result of the occurrence of Conflict; and
b. women victims of Violence.
(3) The provision of services to women as referred to in paragraph (1) includes:
a. providing Special Protection;
b. providing services to women victims of Violence;
c. providing services for the fulfillment of specific basic needs for women victims as a result of the occurrence of Conflict; and
d. improvement of facilities needed by women in Conflict.
(4) In the provision of services as referred to in paragraph (1) the Regency Regional Government is obligated to provide data on women victims of Conflict in the Region.
(5) Special Protection as referred to in paragraph (3) letter a includes efforts to rescue and protect:
a. women so that they do not experience Violence; and
b. the defense of women's human rights.
(6) The provision of services to women victims of Violence as referred to in paragraph (3) letter b includes services for:
a. complaints;
b. health rehabilitation;
c. reproductive health;
d. Social Rehabilitation;
e. Social Reintegration; and
f. legal assistance and/or accompaniment.
(7) Improvement of facilities needed by women in Conflict as referred to in paragraph (3) letter d includes:
a. improvement of health service facilities;
b. improvement of reproductive health service facilities;
c. provision of shelter and housing that is proper, safe, and Gender Responsive;
d. facilitation in the improvement of new housing construction, public facilities and infrastructure; and
e. provision of clean water and sanitation for women.

Article 40
(1) The achievement of KTP prevention involving parties in the Regency Area as referred to in Article 37 letter a is measured by criteria.
(2) The criteria for achievement of KTP prevention as referred to in paragraph (1) consist of:
a. compliance with the implementation of policies regarding KTP prevention;
b. impact of the implementation of programs, activities, and budget regarding KTP prevention;
c. impact of communication, information, and education activities regarding KTP prevention;
d. synergy of programs related to KTP prevention;
e. integration of KTP prevention into the Regency regional development planning; and
f. Community Participation and cooperation with the Business Sector and mass media in KTP prevention

Part Six Protection of Women with Disabilities
Article 34
(1) Protection of women with disabilities is carried out through the fulfillment of the rights of women as persons with disabilities.
(2) The rights of women as persons with disabilities as referred to in paragraph (1), include:
a. life;
b. freedom from stigma;
c. privacy;
d. justice and legal protection;
e. education;
f. employment, entrepreneurship, and cooperatives;
g. health;
h. politics;
i. religion;
j. sports;
k. culture and tourism;
l. social welfare;
m. accessibility;
n. public services;
o. protection from disasters;
p. habilitation and rehabilitation;
q. Concessions;
r. data collection;
s. living independently and being involved in society;
t. expressing, communicating, and obtaining information;
u. freedom of movement and citizenship; and
v. freedom from acts of Discrimination, neglect, torture, and Exploitation.
(3) In addition to having the rights of Women as persons with disabilities as referred to in paragraph (2), women with disabilities have the right:
a. to reproductive health;
b. to accept or reject the use of contraceptives;
c. to receive greater protection from layered Discrimination; and
d. to receive greater protection from acts of Violence, including sexual Violence and Exploitation.

Article 10
(1) The institutionalization of PUG within the Regency Regional Apparatus as referred to in Article 7 letter b, shall be implemented in accordance with standards and procedures.
(2) The PUG Institutionalization Standards as referred to in paragraph (1), consist of:
a. Regent's committee;
b. policies and programs;
c. institutional arrangements;
d. resources;
e. Sex-Disaggregated Data and Gender statistics;
f. implementation methods; and
g. Community Participation.
(3) The PUG Institutionalization Procedures as referred to in paragraph (1) consist of:
a. coordination and synchronization of policy formulation for PUG implementation in the Regency;
b. coordination and synchronization of PUG implementation in the Regency;
c. policy advocacy and assistance for PUG implementation in the Regency including PPRG under Regency authority; and
d. socialization of policies for PUG implementation in the Regency including PPRG under Regency authority.
(4) The coordination and synchronization of policy formulation for PUG implementation in the Regency as referred to in paragraph (3) letter a, includes:
a. harmonization and synchronization of laws and regulations, Regency legal products, and operational policies regarding PUG; and
b. preparation of RPJPD, RPJMD, RKPD, Renstra, and Renja as well as Gender Responsive budgets for Regency Regional Apparatus.
(5) The coordination and synchronization of PUG implementation in the Regency as referred to in paragraph (3) letter b, includes:
a. establishment of PUG Working Group;
b. preparation of annual and 5 (five) year PUG Working Group action plans;
c. PUG Working Group coordination meetings; and
d. integration of Sex-Disaggregated Data into the Gender and Children Data System.
(6) Policy advocacy and assistance for PUG implementation in the Regency as referred to in paragraph (3) letter c, includes:
a. technical coordination meetings for PUG implementation;
b. technical guidance and assistance for PPRG in preparing Gender Responsive programs, activities, and budgets; and
c. facilitation in formulating Gender Responsive Regency policies.
(7) Socialization of policies for PUG implementation in the Regency as referred to in paragraph (3) letter d, includes:
a. dissemination of PUG and PPRG implementation policies to stakeholders; and
b. strengthening of Community Participation in PUG implementation.

Article 77
Strengthening and development of Service Provider Institutions for AMPK in Regency Areas as referred to in Article 70 letter c shall be implemented through:
a. capacity building of institutional resources of service provider institutions for handling AMPK in Regency Areas;
b. provision of special needs assistance for AMPK in Regency Areas;
c. provision of communication, information, and education on AMPK protection in Regency Areas;
d. strengthening networks among service provider institutions for AMPK protection in Regency Areas;
e. facilitation of standardization implementation of service provider institutions for AMPK protection in Regency Areas; and
f. development of service provider institutions for AMPK protection in Regency Areas.

Article 92
(1) The provision of services for Families in realizing Gender Equality and Children's Rights in Regency Areas shall be implemented in accordance with standards and procedures.
(2) The standards for the provision of services for Families as referred to in paragraph (1) include:
 a. institutional matters;
 b. human resources;
 c. facilities and infrastructure;
 d. availability of communication, information, education materials, and modules;
 e. service delivery, including prevention of underage marriage; and
 f. synergy and coordination.
(3) The procedures for the provision of services for Families as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out through the implementation of comprehensive service provision for Families in realizing Gender Equality and Children's Rights in Regency Areas.
(4) Further provisions regarding the implementation of services and the prevention of underage marriage shall be regulated further in a Regent Regulation.

Article 71
Prevention of VAC involving parties in the Regency Area as referred to in Article 70 letter a is implemented through:
a. implementation of policies, programs, and activities for VAC prevention involving parties in the Regency Area;
b. provision of education on VAC prevention activities involving parties in the Regency Area;
c. enhancement of the participation of the Community, Business Sector, media, and Children in VAC prevention in the Regency Area; and
d. strengthening of cooperation to realize CFC, child-friendly sub-districts, and child-friendly Villages/urban villages.

Part Three Fulfillment of Children's Rights
Paragraph 1 General
Article 52
(1) The Regency Regional Government has the obligation and responsibility in the implementation of the Fulfillment of Children's Rights.
(2) The Fulfillment of Children's Rights as referred to in paragraph (1), includes:
a. the right to live, grow, develop, and participate properly in accordance with human dignity and worth, and to receive protection from violence and Discrimination;
b. the right to a name as personal identity and citizenship status;
c. the right to worship according to their religion, to think, and to express themselves in accordance with their level of intelligence and age under the guidance of Parents or Guardians;
d. the right to know their Parents, to be raised, and to be cared for by their own Parents;
e. the right to be cared for or adopted as a foster child or adopted child by others in the event that for any reason the Parents cannot guarantee the child's growth and development, or the Child is in a state of neglect;
f. the right to obtain health services and social security in accordance with physical, mental, spiritual, and social needs;
g. the right to obtain education and teaching in the context of developing their personality and level of intelligence in accordance with their interests and talents;
h. the right to receive protection in educational units from sexual crimes and Violence committed by educators, educational personnel, fellow students, and/or other parties;
i. the right to express and have their opinion heard, to receive, seek, and provide information in accordance with their level of intelligence and age for their self-development in accordance with the values of decency and propriety;
j. the right to rest and utilize leisure time, to associate with children of the same age, to play, to recreate, and to create in accordance with their interests, talents, and level of intelligence for self-development;
k. the right to receive protection from Discrimination, Exploitation, both economic and sexual, neglect, cruelty, violence, and abuse, injustice, and other mistreatment while in the care of Parents, Guardians, or any other party responsible for care;
l. the right to be cared for by their own Parents, unless there are valid reasons and/or legal rules showing that the separation and/or divorce is in the best interests of the Child and is a last consideration;
m. the right to meet directly and maintain personal relations regularly with both Parents, to receive care, maintenance, education and protection for the growth and development process from both Parents in accordance with their abilities, talents, and interests, to obtain living expenses from both Parents, and to obtain other Children's Rights, in the event of separation and/or divorce as referred to in letter l;
n. the right to obtain protection from abuse in political activities, involvement in armed conflicts, involvement in social unrest, involvement in events containing elements of Violence, involvement in warfare, and sexual crimes;
o. the right to obtain protection from being targets of persecution, torture, or imposition of inhumane punishment, and to obtain freedom in accordance with the law;
p. the right to receive humane treatment and placement separated from adults, to obtain legal assistance or other assistance effectively at every stage of applicable legal efforts, and to defend themselves and obtain justice before an objective and impartial juvenile court in closed sessions to the public, in the case of Children deprived of their freedom;
q. the right to confidentiality in the case of Children who become victims or perpetrators of sexual Violence or who are in conflict with the law;
r. the right to receive legal assistance and other assistance in the case of children becoming victims or perpetrators of criminal acts; and
s. the right to receive protection and maintenance after divorce.
(3) The implementation of the Fulfillment of Children's Rights in the Regency Region as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2), shall be carried out in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations governing the protection of Children.

Article 57
Strengthening and development of Institutions Providing Services for Improving the Quality of Life of Children in Regency Areas as referred to in Article 53 letter b shall be implemented through:
a. capacity building of resources of Institutions Providing Services for Improving the Quality of Life of Children in Regency Areas;
b. assistance in the implementation of services for improving the quality of life of Children in Regency Areas;
c. development of materials and implementation of communication, information, and education on the Fulfillment of Children's Rights for Institutions Providing Services for Improving the Quality of Life of Children in Regency Areas;
d. strengthening networks among Institutions Providing Services for Improving the Quality of Life of Children in Regency Areas;
e. facilitation of the implementation of standardization of Institutions Providing Services for Improving the Quality of Life of Children in Regency Areas; and
f. development of Institutions Providing Services for Improving the Quality of Life of Children in Regency Areas.

Article 69
The District Regional Government in implementing the provision of Special Protection for Children in the District as referred to in Article 61, shall provide:
a. social workers and social welfare personnel;
b. competent and trained health personnel;
c. officers …
d. spiritual/worship guidance officers;
e. educators and education personnel; and/or
f. legal aid personnel.

Article 47
(1) The strengthening and development of Women's Protection Service Provider Institutions in Regency Areas as referred to in Article 37 letter c shall be implemented in accordance with standards and procedures.
(2) The standards for strengthening and development as referred to in paragraph (1) include:
a. measurable and certified human resource capacity of Women's Protection Service Provider Institutions for women victims of Violence in Regency Areas;
b. specific needs for women in emergency situations and special conditions in Regency Areas that are well-targeted and effective;
c. effective and efficient communication, information, and education strategies regarding the protection of women's rights;
d. networks among Women's Protection Service Provider Institutions in Regency Areas;
e. Women's Protection Service Provider Institutions in Regency Areas have agreed service standards and operational system procedures; and
f. Measurable development of Women's Protection Service Provider Institutions in Regency Areas.
(3) The procedures for strengthening and development as referred to in paragraph (1) include:
a. policy advocacy and assistance in providing service facilities and infrastructure for women victims of Violence in Regency Areas;
b. capacity building of institutional resources of service provider institutions for handling women victims of Violence in Regency Areas;
c. provision of specific needs for women in emergency situations and special conditions in Regency Areas; and
d. strengthening networks among Women's Protection Service Provider Institutions in Regency Areas.

Article 46
Strengthening and development of Women's Protection Service Provider Institutions in Regency Areas as referred to in Article 37 letter c shall be implemented through:
a. capacity building of human resources of Women's Protection Service Provider Institutions for women victims of Violence in Regency Areas;
b. provision of specific needs for women in emergency situations and special conditions in Regency Areas;
c. development of communication, information, and education strategies regarding the protection of women's rights in Regency Areas;
d. strengthening networks among Women's Protection Service Provider Institutions in Regency Areas;
e. facilitation of efforts to fulfill standards of Women's Protection Service Provider Institutions in Regency Areas; and
f. development of Women's Protection Service Provider Institutions in Regency Areas.

Article 40
(1) The achievement of KTP prevention involving parties in the Regency Area as referred to in Article 37 letter a is measured by criteria.
(2) The criteria for achievement of KTP prevention as referred to in paragraph (1) consist of:
a. compliance with the implementation of policies regarding KTP prevention;
b. impact of the implementation of programs, activities, and budget regarding KTP prevention;
c. impact of communication, information, and education activities regarding KTP prevention;
d. synergy of programs related to KTP prevention;
e. integration of KTP prevention into the Regency regional development planning; and
f. Community Participation and cooperation with the Business Sector and mass media in KTP prevention

Article 54
Institutionalization of the Fulfillment of Children’s Rights in government institutions, non-governmental institutions, and the business sector in the Regency area as referred to in Article 53 letter a is implemented through:
a. implementation of policies, programs, and activities for the Fulfillment of Children’s Rights in efforts to prevent violations of children’s rights in government institutions, non-governmental institutions, media, and the business sector in the Regency area;
b. increasing participation and the role of the Children’s Forum or the like in the Regency area;
c. strengthening the institutions and resources of government institutions, non-governmental institutions, media, and the business sector in the Regency area;
d. strengthening networks among government institutions, non-governmental institutions, media, and the business sector in the Regency area; and
e. strengthening cross-institutional cooperation among government institutions, non-governmental institutions, media, and the business sector to realize KLA, a Child-Friendly Sub-district, and a Child-Friendly Village/Urban Village.

Article 10
(1) The institutionalization of PUG within the Regency Regional Apparatus as referred to in Article 7 letter b, shall be implemented in accordance with standards and procedures.
(2) The PUG Institutionalization Standards as referred to in paragraph (1), consist of:
a. Regent's committee;
b. policies and programs;
c. institutional arrangements;
d. resources;
e. Sex-Disaggregated Data and Gender statistics;
f. implementation methods; and
g. Community Participation.
(3) The PUG Institutionalization Procedures as referred to in paragraph (1) consist of:
a. coordination and synchronization of policy formulation for PUG implementation in the Regency;
b. coordination and synchronization of PUG implementation in the Regency;
c. policy advocacy and assistance for PUG implementation in the Regency including PPRG under Regency authority; and
d. socialization of policies for PUG implementation in the Regency including PPRG under Regency authority.
(4) The coordination and synchronization of policy formulation for PUG implementation in the Regency as referred to in paragraph (3) letter a, includes:
a. harmonization and synchronization of laws and regulations, Regency legal products, and operational policies regarding PUG; and
b. preparation of RPJPD, RPJMD, RKPD, Renstra, and Renja as well as Gender Responsive budgets for Regency Regional Apparatus.
(5) The coordination and synchronization of PUG implementation in the Regency as referred to in paragraph (3) letter b, includes:
a. establishment of PUG Working Group;
b. preparation of annual and 5 (five) year PUG Working Group action plans;
c. PUG Working Group coordination meetings; and
d. integration of Sex-Disaggregated Data into the Gender and Children Data System.
(6) Policy advocacy and assistance for PUG implementation in the Regency as referred to in paragraph (3) letter c, includes:
a. technical coordination meetings for PUG implementation;
b. technical guidance and assistance for PPRG in preparing Gender Responsive programs, activities, and budgets; and
c. facilitation in formulating Gender Responsive Regency policies.
(7) Socialization of policies for PUG implementation in the Regency as referred to in paragraph (3) letter d, includes:
a. dissemination of PUG and PPRG implementation policies to stakeholders; and
b. strengthening of Community Participation in PUG implementation.

Article 55
(1) The Institutionalization of the Fulfillment of Children's Rights in government institutions, non-government institutions, and the Business Sector in Regency Areas as referred to in Article 53 letter a shall be implemented in accordance with standards and procedures.
(2) The institutionalization standards for the Fulfillment of Children's Rights in government institutions, non-government institutions, and the Business Sector in Regency Areas as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.
(3) The institutionalization procedures for the Fulfillment of Children's Rights as referred to in paragraph (1) shall consist of:
a. policy advocacy and assistance for the Fulfillment of Children's Rights in government institutions, non-government institutions, media, and the Business Sector in Regency Areas, covering:
1. harmonization and synchronization of laws and regulations and legal products of Regency Areas related to the rights of Children and efforts to Fulfill Children's Rights;
2. preparation of RPJPD, RPJMD, RKPD, Renstra, and Renja documents with a Children's rights perspective;
3. technical coordination meetings for the implementation of policies, strategies and programs, as well as monitoring and evaluation of efforts to Fulfill Children's Rights;
4. technical guidance and assistance in the preparation of programs, activities, and budgets that are responsive to Children's Rights and their implementation;
5. facilitation in the preparation of Regency Area policies that are responsive to Children's Rights and their implementation;
6. dissemination of policies on the Fulfillment of Children's Rights to stakeholders; and
7. strengthening of Community Participation in efforts to Fulfill Children's Rights; and
b. coordination and synchronization of the Institutionalization of the Fulfillment of Children's Rights in Regency Areas, covering:
1. establishment of working groups, focal points, technical teams, and task forces for the Fulfillment of Children's Rights;
2. preparation of work plans for working groups, focal points, technical teams, and task forces for the Fulfillment of Children's Rights;
3. coordination meetings to strengthen working groups, focal points, technical teams, and task forces for the Fulfillment of Children's Rights; and
4. integration of data on the situation and condition of Children by gender in the Fulfillment of Children's Rights into a data system that can be accessed through official publication media.

Second Part Implementation
Paragraph 1 General
Article 7
The implementation of Women's Quality of Life in the Regency Area as referred to in Article 6 paragraph (1), consists of:
a. implementation of PUG in the Regency Area;
b. Institutionalization of PUG within the Regency Regional Apparatus;
c. Women's Empowerment in the political, legal, social, and economic fields in Community Organizations in the Regency Area;
d. strengthening and development of Women's Empowerment Service Provider Institutions in the Regency Area; and
e. Capacity Building of Women in the Regency Area.

Paragraph 3 Institutionalization of PUG within the Regency Regional Apparatus
Article 9
(1) Institutionalization of PUG within the Regency Regional Apparatus as referred to in Article 7 letter b, shall be implemented through:
a. fulfillment of PUG Institutionalization standards;
b. integration of Gender Perspective;
c. preparation of planning and budgeting; and
d. advocacy, facilitating, and providing technical assistance.
(2) Integration of Gender Perspective as referred to in paragraph (1) letter b, shall be incorporated into the policies and development programs of the Regency.
(3) Policies and development programs of the Regency as referred to in paragraph (2), shall consist of:
a. RPJPD;
b. RPJMD;
c. RKPD;
d. Renstra; and
e. Renja.
(4) Policies and development programs of the Regency as referred to in paragraph (2), shall include:
a. planning;
b. formulation;
c. implementation;
d. monitoring;
e. controlling; and
f. evaluation.
(5) Preparation of planning and budgeting as referred to in paragraph (1) letter c, shall consider equal Access, Participation, control, and Benefits between women and men, taking into account the specific aspirations, needs, and problems of women and men.
(6) Advocacy, facilitating and providing technical assistance as referred to in paragraph (1) letter d, shall be directed to institutions having legislative functions and vertical agencies in the Regency related to the implementation of PUG.

Article 43
(1) The provision of services for women victims of Violence requiring coordination in the Regency Region as referred to in Article 37 letter b shall be implemented in accordance with standards and procedures.
(2) The standards for provision of services for women victims of Violence requiring coordination in the Regency Region as referred to in paragraph (1) include:
a. receiving reports and/or outreach to victims of Violence;
b. providing information about the rights of victims of Violence;
c. facilitating the provision of health services;
d. facilitating the provision of psychological strengthening services;
e. facilitating the provision of psychosocial services, Social Rehabilitation, social empowerment, Repatriation, and Social Reintegration;
f. providing legal services;
g. identifying economic empowerment needs;
h. identifying temporary shelter needs for victims of Violence and Families of victims of Violence that need to be fulfilled immediately;
i. facilitating the needs of Victims of Violence with disabilities;
j. coordination and cooperation for the fulfillment of the rights of victims of Violence with other institutions; and
k. monitoring the fulfillment of the rights of victims of Violence by law enforcement apparatus during the judicial process.
(3) The procedures for provision of services as referred to in paragraph (1) consist of:
a. provision of public complaint services for women victims of Violence in the Regency Region, including:
1. establishment of UPTD PPA in the Regency Region;
2. implementation of UPTD PPA services; and
3. provision and strengthening of human resources of service providers through training and certification of Women's rights protection services.
b. coordination and synchronization of the implementation of provision of further referral services for women victims of violence in the Regency Region, including:
1. cross-coordination of Regency Regional Apparatus for the implementation of provision of KTP case services;
2. development of policies and implementation of an integrated KTP case handling system across related Regency Regional Apparatus; and
3. data collection, monitoring, and evaluation of KTP case handling.
